# (level, question, expected response, tone, if you do not know)
NONTECH = [
("Basic", "Please introduce yourself and tell the panel briefly what your study is about.",
 '"Thank you, Professor. My name is ... and I am presenting my MSc thesis, Machine Learning Survival Prediction for ICU Patients with Non-Communicable Diseases. The study asks a simple design question: when we build a survival model for ICU patients with an NCD, should we train it on all ICU patients, on NCD patients only, or start from the general model and adapt it to NCD patients through transfer learning? I compared the three approaches on MIMIC-IV, using Cox, random survival forest and XGBoost, all tested on the same held-out group of NCD patients."',
 "Warm and composed. Smile, keep it under one minute and end on the question your study answers.",
 '"I will keep the introduction short so we have more time for questions." (This one you should never fail; rehearse it until it is automatic.)'),
("Basic", "What is the title of your thesis, and why did you word it that way?",
 '"The title is Machine Learning Survival Prediction for ICU Patients with Non-Communicable Diseases. Each part names something I had to decide: machine learning is the family of methods, survival prediction is the outcome, so time to death rather than yes or no, and ICU patients with NCDs is the population. The comparison of general, disease-specific and transfer learning approaches is the core of the study."',
 "Confident and plain. Walk through the title word by word.",
 '"I chose each word to match a part of the design; if the panel feels a word is misleading, I am happy to revise it."'),
("Basic", "What motivated you to choose this topic?",
 '"Thank you for the question. NCDs cause about three in four deaths not linked to COVID-19, and many of these patients end up in intensive care. When I read the ICU prediction literature, almost every model was trained on all ICU patients together. I wanted to know whether that habit actually serves NCD patients, because it affects how hospitals, including those in low- and middle-income countries, choose prediction tools."',
 "Genuine and personal, but brief. One fact, one observation, one reason.",
 'Not applicable: this is about you. If your mind goes blank, say: "Let me give the practical reason first," and state the gap.'),
("Basic", "In one sentence, what problem does your study address?",
 '"It is not known whether a general, a disease-specific or a transfer learning survival model predicts survival best for ICU patients with NCDs, because no identified study had compared the three on the same held-out patients."',
 "Crisp. Say it in one breath, then stop and let the panel follow up.",
 '"Put simply, we did not know which way of building the model works best for these patients."'),
("Basic", "What were your main objective and your specific objectives?",
 '"My main objective was to compare the predictive performance of general, disease-specific and transfer learning survival models for one-year survival among ICU patients with NCDs, using MIMIC-IV. The four specific objectives followed the order of the work: first construct the analysis dataset, then develop the Cox, random survival forest and XGBoost models under the three approaches, then evaluate them on the held-out group of NCD patients, and finally test whether the ranking holds for discharge home alive and make a recommendation."',
 "Structured. Count them on your fingers if it helps you keep order.",
 '"May I refer to my objectives slide so that I state them exactly as written?" Asking to check is better than misquoting your own thesis.'),
("Basic", "Who benefits from your research?",
 '"Thank you. Three groups mainly. Model developers and researchers get evidence on whether to train on all ICU patients, on NCD patients only, or to use transfer learning. Clinicians get survival estimates that fit NCD patients more closely. Hospitals and policy makers, especially where resources are limited, learn whether one adapted model is enough or whether separate models are worth the cost."',
 "Practical and modest. Avoid claiming the study will change practice everywhere.",
 '"The clearest beneficiaries are researchers who build these models; the clinical benefit would need further validation before I would claim it."'),
("Intermediate", "How did you identify the research gap?",
 '"I read three bodies of work. First, general ICU mortality models, which are mostly trained on all patients. Second, disease-specific models for heart failure, sepsis, stroke and cancer, which report results only within their own disease group. Third, the small transfer learning literature, such as Xu and colleagues on eICU, which used single diagnoses and a yes-or-no outcome. None of them compared all three approaches for NCD patients with a survival outcome on a common held-out group. That intersection is the gap."',
 "Methodical. Show that the gap came from reading, not from assumption.",
 '"I cannot claim to have read every paper in the field, so I phrase it as no identified study, based on my search of the databases I listed in Chapter 2."'),
("Intermediate", "How did you select your literature, and how current is it?",
 '"I searched for ICU mortality and survival prediction, disease-specific ICU models, transfer learning in clinical prediction, and survival methods. I kept peer-reviewed sources wherever possible and favoured work from 2021 onwards; in Chapter 1 about 85% of the references are from 2021 to 2026. The older references are the seminal method papers, such as Cox in 1972 and Ishwaran on random survival forests in 2008."',
 "Factual. Give the proportion if you know it; numbers reassure examiners.",
 '"I do not have the exact search counts with me, but the search strategy is described in Chapter 2 and I can send the full log after the defence."'),
("Intermediate", "What ethical issues did your study raise, and how did you deal with them?",
 '"Thank you. The data are real patient records, even though they are de-identified. I completed the PhysioNet credentialing and training and signed the data use agreement. I kept all patient-level data and trained models on my own machine, never on GitHub, because models can memorise records. Only aggregate tables and figures were shared. I also report that survival predictions should support, not replace, clinical judgement, because a poor prognosis estimate could influence end-of-life decisions."',
 "Serious and responsible. Ethics questions reward calm, concrete answers.",
 '"I am not sure of the exact institutional procedure for that point; I followed the PhysioNet agreement and my university\'s guidance, and I would confirm the rest with the ethics office."'),
("Intermediate", "What is your main contribution to knowledge?",
 '"The main contribution is a direct, fair comparison of general, disease-specific and transfer learning survival models for ICU patients with NCDs, all tested on the same held-out group. Earlier work either compared approaches for single diagnoses, used a yes-or-no outcome, or never tested a general model on the disease group. A second contribution is the reusable design itself: the same pipeline can be applied to any other patient group in MIMIC-IV."',
 "Confident but not boastful. State it, then stop.",
 '"If I had to reduce it to one line: we now have evidence, not assumption, on which approach to use for this group."'),
("Intermediate", "What was the biggest challenge you faced, and how did you overcome it?",
 '"The biggest challenge was changing direction mid-way. I first built binary in-hospital mortality models, and my supervisor advised moving to survival analysis, which is a better fit for the question. I kept the cohort and predictor pipeline, relabelled the earlier outputs as legacy, and rebuilt the outcome and models around time to death. A practical challenge was the size of the MIMIC-IV event tables, which I handled by filtering them once with DuckDB and caching the result."',
 "Honest and solution-focused. Examiners like hearing how you recovered, not how hard it was.",
 '"There were several; the one that taught me most was ..." Pick any real one rather than freezing.'),
("Intermediate", "How did your study change from the original proposal?",
 '"Thank you for asking. The original proposal predicted in-hospital death as a yes-or-no outcome with logistic regression, random forest and XGBoost. After discussion with my supervisor, I moved to survival analysis with a one-year horizon, using Cox, random survival forest and XGBoost survival models. The research question, the cohort and the three approaches stayed the same; the outcome and methods changed. The earlier results are kept in the repository as legacy outputs and are not presented as the main findings."',
 "Open. A change of direction is normal; present it as a deliberate improvement.",
 '"The full history is in my research plan document; I can walk the panel through it if useful."'),
("Intermediate", "What are the main limitations of your study?",
 '"There are three I would highlight. MIMIC-IV comes from one hospital in Boston, so the findings may not transfer directly to other settings, including Kenya. The data are retrospective, so some predictors such as bilirubin, pH and lactate are missing for many patients. And two NCD groups, chronic respiratory disease and diabetes, are small. For each one I describe the mitigation in Section 1.8: full cohort reporting, a documented missing-data method, and results reported per NCD group."',
 "Calm and non-defensive. Name the limitation, then the mitigation.",
 '"Those are the ones I identified; if the panel sees another, I would welcome it and add it to the final version."'),
("Intermediate", "If you started this study again, what would you do differently?",
 '"I would start with the survival framing from day one rather than moving to it later, which would have saved time. I would also plan external validation from the beginning, for example by applying for eICU access early, so the model could be tested on a second database."',
 "Reflective and mature. Two concrete changes are enough.",
 '"Honestly, I would need to think about that more carefully, but one thing I already know is ..."'),
("Intermediate", "How do your findings compare with previous studies?",
 'Tie your answer to your actual results. Structure: "Thank you. Xu and colleagues found that transfer learning beat both diagnosis-specific and pooled models on eICU. My results [agree / partly agree / disagree] for NCD patients with a survival outcome: [state the ranking]. On the choice of method, Huang and colleagues found machine learning no better than Cox in cancer survival, and in my study [state whether Cox, random survival forest or XGBoost led]."',
 "Balanced. Agreeing with the literature is reassuring; disagreeing is fine if you explain why.",
 '"I would need to check the exact figures in that paper before comparing directly, but in broad terms ..."'),
("Advanced", "How would you convince a sceptical policy maker that your findings matter?",
 '"I would talk in terms of choices and cost. Every hospital that adopts a prediction tool must decide whether one general model is enough or whether NCD patients need their own. My study gives evidence for that decision. If a general or adapted model performs as well as a separate one, a health system saves the cost of building and maintaining extra models. If not, it knows where investment is justified."',
 "Persuasive but grounded. Speak to their interests: money, staff time, patient safety.",
 '"I am not a policy specialist, so I would frame the evidence and leave the policy decision to those who know the local system."'),
("Advanced", "How generalisable are your findings to Kenya or other African settings?",
 '"Thank you, this is an important point. Directly, they are limited. MIMIC-IV reflects one US academic centre, with different patient mix, staffing and resources. What does transfer is the design and the question. A Kenyan hospital with electronic records could run the same comparison on its own patients. I report the cohort characteristics in full so readers can judge how close their population is."',
 "Honest. Do not oversell; examiners respect a clear boundary.",
 '"I do not have Kenyan ICU data to test this, which is exactly why I list it as a limitation and a direction for future work."'),
("Advanced", "What recommendations do you make, and to whom?",
 'Base this on your results. Structure: "To model developers, I recommend [the winning approach] for NCD patients, because [the evidence]. To researchers, I recommend reporting performance separately for disease groups, not only for the whole ICU population. To hospitals, I recommend validating any model locally before use."',
 "Decisive. Recommendations should sound like conclusions, not guesses.",
 '"My recommendation is limited to what the evidence supports; beyond that I would call it a suggestion for future work."'),
("Advanced", "What future research would you suggest?",
 '"First, external validation on a second database such as eICU, and ideally on African ICU data. Second, separate models for each NCD group once larger samples are available, especially for chronic respiratory disease and diabetes. Third, testing other transfer learning methods and deep learning survival models such as DeepSurv. Fourth, prospective evaluation to see whether the predictions change clinical decisions."',
 "Forward-looking and specific.",
 '"Beyond those I have listed, I would welcome the panel\'s suggestions."'),
("Advanced", "In one minute, what should this panel remember about your thesis?",
 '"Three things. First, the question: whether ICU survival models for NCD patients should be general, disease-specific or transfer learning. Second, the design: three methods, three approaches, one common held-out group, so the comparison is fair. Third, the answer: [state your main finding in one sentence] and what it means for how such models should be built."',
 "Strong close. Slow down and make eye contact with each examiner.",
 'Rehearse this. If you lose your place, say "The key point is ..." and give your main finding.'),
]
